  "dom_id": "step-q156179",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.",
  "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$",
